  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nSevere weather, including damaging wind gusts and a few tornadoes, \nremains a concern for this afternoon and evening as a well \nadvertised strong system approaches from the west. At 08Z today, \nwater vapor imagery showed a deep mid-level trough stretching from \ncentral Montana into the Four Corners region, and a corridor of high\nwater vapor content into western KS and the Northern Plains within \nthe southwest flow ahead of the trough axis. H5 and H7 VWP at DDC \nsampled a 50+ kt jet. Sfc low pressure was noted over the far \nnortheast Colorado and southwest Nebraska stateline, and a cold \nfront trailing to the south. Morning and midday impacts from this \nsystem as it progresses east-northeast will be increasing sfc winds \nand an initial round or two of rain. Sfc gusts quickly pick up this \nmorning and are likely to reach 40 to 45 mph through midday. A batch \nof rain will also move west to east through the area this morning, \nreaching far eastern KS around 18Z. Showers may mix down stronger \nwinds aloft, but severe weather is not expected until later in the\nday.\n\nMoisture advection increases dew points into the upper 60s ahead of \nthe cold front this afternoon, but mixed layer instability still \nappears to be limited by cloud cover and is expected to remain less \nthan 1000 J/kg. However, given the strong wind field associated with \nthis system, the environment within the warm sector will consist of \naround 50 kts of effective shear and 200+ m2/s2 of 0-3 km SRH. So, \nby mid-afternoon, more robust thunderstorm development is expected \nin central/south-central KS. Any initial discrete supercells could \nquickly evolve into clusters or line segments as they quickly race \nnorth-northeast, and eventually into far eastern KS this evening. \nThe tornado threat will be greatest where storms can remain discrete,\nbut there is also at least a low probability of QLCS spin-ups given \nthe magnitude of the bulk shear parameters. Damaging winds will also \nbe a concern, especially with bowing line segments. It is also worth \nmentioning the heavy rainfall potential with pwats expected to exceed\nthe daily max of 1.4 inches, however fast storm motion should limit \nthe flooding threat. The severe threat should then diminish into the\nlate evening hours as instability wanes.\n\nThe cold front doesn't entirely move through the area until midday \nTuesday with the upper low spinning over the area. A few lingering \nshowers are possible and temperatures will trend cooler and more \nseasonable into the middle of the week. Even so, nighttime \ntemperatures look to remain above freezing for the foreseeable \nfuture, and POPs remain less than 10 percent until this weekend.",
